WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.838 --> 00:00:06.190
Under this street lies the medium-voltage grid: cables of 10 or 20 kilovolts,

2
00:00:06.329 --> 00:00:10.300
running from a substation past a row of secondary substations.

3
00:00:11.022 --> 00:00:15.225
Each one steps the medium voltage down to low voltage for the homes.

4
00:00:16.285 --> 00:00:18.085
At seven forty it goes wrong.

5
00:00:18.844 --> 00:00:22.037
A cable fault trips the protection in the substation,

6
00:00:22.165 --> 00:00:25.416
where high voltage is stepped down to medium voltage.

7
00:00:26.111 --> 00:00:31.115
The feeder breaker, the big switch at the start of one outgoing cable route, opens.

8
00:00:32.145 --> 00:00:35.686
The control room watches and switches the grid around the clock.

9
00:00:36.485 --> 00:00:41.791
It sees the trip within seconds in SCADA, its remote monitoring and control system.

10
00:00:42.794 --> 00:00:49.365
Behind this feeder hang 12 secondary substations, with 1,800 customers between them.

11
00:00:50.034 --> 00:00:51.694
They are all off supply now.

12
00:00:53.045 --> 00:00:54.740
Low voltage is different.

13
00:00:55.498 --> 00:01:00.653
The grid in the street, 400 and 230 volts, is barely measured.

14
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:06.605
You often notice a fault here only when customers call the national outage number.

15
00:01:07.355 --> 00:01:08.075
More and more,

16
00:01:08.191 --> 00:01:11.151
smart meters and sensors in the stations also

17
00:01:11.209 --> 00:01:13.903
report that a street has lost its voltage.

18
00:01:14.935 --> 00:01:18.302
And every minute before you know counts in the outage time.

19
00:01:19.035 --> 00:01:22.913
How far to go with sensors is a debate: cheap for one station,

20
00:01:23.075 --> 00:01:25.304
expensive across tens of thousands.

21
00:01:26.307 --> 00:01:31.033
So ask: how do we find out about an LV fault today, and how long does that take?

22
00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:33.914
Back to medium voltage.

23
00:01:34.619 --> 00:01:37.602
MV grids are built as rings, but run open.

24
00:01:38.646 --> 00:01:41.932
One switch stays open: the normally open point.

25
00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:45.768
That is the switch in an MV ring that is kept open;

26
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.833
you close it to feed a section from the other side.

27
00:01:49.538 --> 00:01:53.172
Here it sits in station 12, towards a neighbouring ring.

28
00:01:54.169 --> 00:01:56.607
So each feeder is fed from one side.

29
00:01:57.368 --> 00:02:01.954
That keeps fault currents and protection simple, while leaving a backup route.

30
00:02:03.572 --> 00:02:07.125
At a quarter past eight the fault technician is on site.

31
00:02:07.891 --> 00:02:11.246
With the control room, he first finds where the fault is.

32
00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.954
For that, the stations have fault indicators:

33
00:02:15.058 --> 00:02:18.599
small devices that flag whether fault current passed through.

34
00:02:19.619 --> 00:02:24.947
The indicators in stations 1 to 5 have triggered, the one in station 6 has not.

35
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.694
So the fault drew current past station 5, but not past 6.

36
00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:35.342
The fault lies between the last station that flagged and the first that did not:

37
00:02:35.481 --> 00:02:38.209
here in the cable between 5 and 6.

38
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.303
Without indicators you search by switching

39
00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:44.171
sections off and trying to re-energise them.

40
00:02:44.939 --> 00:02:46.019
That takes longer.

41
00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:51.846
Usually the control room gives the switching instructions, step by step,

42
00:02:52.032 --> 00:02:54.516
and the technician confirms each step.

43
00:02:55.271 --> 00:02:59.184
He opens the load-break switch in station 5 towards 6,

44
00:02:59.358 --> 00:03:02.028
and in station 6 the one towards 5.

45
00:03:02.727 --> 00:03:06.849
The damaged cable section is now isolated on both sides.

46
00:03:07.888 --> 00:03:09.827
Then the feeder breaker recloses.

47
00:03:10.633 --> 00:03:13.094
Stations 1 to 5 have power again.

48
00:03:14.103 --> 00:03:17.285
For the rest, the normally open point must close.

49
00:03:17.998 --> 00:03:20.599
First the control room checks that the neighbouring

50
00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.955
ring can carry the extra morning load.

51
00:03:24.009 --> 00:03:27.434
It can, so the open point in station 12 closes.

52
00:03:28.155 --> 00:03:31.429
Now stations 6 to 12 are fed from the other side.

53
00:03:32.156 --> 00:03:35.094
Everyone is back, and nobody waits for the repair,

54
00:03:35.245 --> 00:03:38.612
because the fault is in a cable between two stations.

55
00:03:39.916 --> 00:03:42.946
By hand, with a technician driving to the stations,

56
00:03:43.132 --> 00:03:46.545
most customers are back within one to two hours this way.

57
00:03:47.228 --> 00:03:50.444
With remote-controlled stations it can take minutes.

58
00:03:51.451 --> 00:03:57.580
In this example, 1,800 customers were off supply for an average of 50 minutes.

59
00:03:58.274 --> 00:04:00.898
That makes 90,000 customer minutes.

60
00:04:01.923 --> 00:04:06.219
A customer connected inside the isolated section waits for the repair.

61
00:04:06.969 --> 00:04:10.011
A measurement van pinpoints the fault, the crew digs,

62
00:04:10.127 --> 00:04:12.797
makes one or two joints and tests the cable.

63
00:04:13.505 --> 00:04:17.627
Before they touch the cable, the five safety rules apply: disconnect,

64
00:04:17.766 --> 00:04:23.002
secure against reconnection, verify absence of voltage, earth and short-circuit,

65
00:04:23.222 --> 00:04:25.997
and protect against adjacent live parts.

66
00:04:26.987 --> 00:04:30.678
That often takes a day, and meanwhile the ring has no backup.

67
00:04:31.999 --> 00:04:35.900
Grid operators add up a year of outages in three numbers.

68
00:04:36.604 --> 00:04:40.133
SAIFI is the number of interruptions per customer per year.

69
00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:45.913
CAIDI is the average duration of one interruption, for the customers affected.

70
00:04:46.949 --> 00:04:51.872
Multiply them, and you get SAIDI: the average outage minutes per customer per year.

71
00:04:52.582 --> 00:05:01.220
In 2025 in the Netherlands that was 0.316 interruptions of 73.9 minutes on average:

72
00:05:01.336 --> 00:05:04.552
23.4 minutes from unplanned outages.

73
00:05:05.274 --> 00:05:08.769
Planned work added 27.2 minutes on top.

74
00:05:09.803 --> 00:05:13.843
A pitfall: one long rural feeder can drive a whole region’s SAIDI.
